course: dictionary
lesson: 11811
title: Bickering and small arguments
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: «The twins spent the whole car journey bickering about who had the window seat.» Bickering means arguing again and again about small, unimportant things. Nobody was in danger, and nothing big was at stake. The twins just kept going over one silly point.
explain: Look for clues in the sentence. The word «constant» in «constant bickering» tells you it does not stop. Words like «silly», «petty» or «over» before a small thing are clues too. Ask yourself: is this a big, serious fight, or a small, annoying one?
explain: Bickering is often about feelings as well as facts. «Stop bickering, you two, or nobody gets a biscuit!» Mum is not worried about a real danger. She is fed up with the squabbling and wants it to end.
fact: Bickering means arguing about small, unimportant things, again and again.
fact: «Bicker» is the verb: «The friends bickered over who would go first.»
fact: The verb forms are bicker, bickers, bickered and bickering.
fact: Useful phrases: «constant bickering», «stop bickering», and «bickering over» something small.
fact: The word describes small, annoying arguments, not serious ones.
exercise: «The sisters were bickering over the last slice of cake.» What were they bickering about? | A lost pet | *A small, unimportant thing | A serious danger | A big trip abroad
exercise: Which word means arguing about small things again and again? | Drooped | *Bickering | Barely | Famished
exercise: «Mum sighed at the constant bickering of the boys.» What does constant show? | *It keeps going on and on | It stopped quickly | It was a secret | It happened only once
test: «The class was tired of the bickering over whose turn it was on the computer.» What does this tell us about the argument? | It is a dangerous storm | It is a happy party | It is a secret plan | *It is small and annoying
test: Nadia says, «They were bickering again.» What is she saying? | They were very quiet | *They were arguing about small things again | They had finished their homework | They were singing together
exam: «Stop bickering over nothing, Sam, and help me tidy up.» What does Mum want Sam to do? | Leave the room | Go to bed early | *Stop arguing over small things and help | Sing a song
